Debug.Assert(index >= 0 && index <= _size || index == _size);
</code>
Here `index` is the old `index` value before the removal and `_size` is the size of the array before the removal and `_size + 1` is the new size after removal. The assertion states that the index should be either still valid after removal or have become `_size` which is the next index after the index of the last element. 

<code>
Debug.Assert(Array.IndexOf((Array)_items, value, 0, index + 1) == -1 || Array.IndexOf((Array)_items, value, index + 1, _size - (index + 1)) != -1);
</code>
The assertion checks the validity of the newly added element. It is valid if either it has disappeared (`IndexOf` returns `-1`) before the index `index + 1` or remain valid (`IndexOf` does not return `-1`) after index `index + 1`. This is to ensure that a newly added element does not break the existing order.

<code>
Debug.Assert(Array.IndexOf((Array)_items, value, 0, index) == -1 || _version == versionOld);
</code>
`index` is the old `index` value, `_version` is the version before the element insertion and `versionOld` is the old version. The assertion checks whether elements before the current index and the version have not changed after insertion or the element has been removed and the version did not increase.

<code>
Debug.Assert(Array.Exists((Array)_items, value) || Notp.Assume.IsTrue(index < 0 && index <= _size));
</code>
The `Notp.Assume.IsTrue` is a statement that an element has either been removed or does not exist. Here, if `index` is invalid (less than 0 or greater than or equals to the size), it implies the element has been removed. If `index` is valid, it implies that the new value exists in the list. The `Array.Exists` is a predicate that checks for the existence of specific items in the given array.
<code>
Debug.Assert(index < _items.size);
</code>
The size `_items.size` is the number of elements in the array and `index` is a index number in the array. This assertion states that if `index` is in the array then `index` should be less than the size of the array. If `index` is not in the array then it cannot be less than the size of the array. 

<code>
Debug.Assert(Array.LastIndexOf((Array)_items, value, index, _items.Count - index) != -1 || Array.LastIndexOf((Array)_items, value, 0, index) != -1);
</code>
The assertion checks if an element has disappeared (`IndexOf` returns `-1`) on the left side (before `index`) or on the right side of (after `index`). This is to ensure that a newly added element does not break the existing order.

<code>
Debug.Assert(Array.Exists((Array)_items, value) && index == Array.IndexOf((Array)_items, value, 0, _size) || Notp.Assume.IsTrue(index == -1));
</code>
The assertion checks whether the value exists in the array elements and the index is either the index where it was found or if it does not exist it checks whether the index has been removed. Index is removed when it equals to -1.
<code>
Debug.Assert(NOTP.Assume.ISTRUE(Array.Exists((Array)_items, value)) || index >= 0);
</code>
The assertion checks if a value does exist in the array where it has been removed then the index should be -1 or it should be a valid index.

